DEVLOG — performance pass (ed138b6)

The site rendered at 8-16fps. Profiled with Playwright + a rAF frame
counter, disabling one suspect at a time rather than guessing.

Result: home 8->52, portfolio 7->52, more 12->57, resume 59. Load time was
never the problem; frame cost was.

Five causes, three self-inflicted:
1. scroll-reveal never cleaned up — left filter:blur(0px) and will-change
   on every revealed section permanently. A filter of ANY value pins a
   composited layer and disables subpixel text antialiasing.
2. Viewport-sized backdrop-blur on the layout panel (pre-existing) over the
   animating MatrixRain, so it could never be cached.
3. background-gradient animated background-position (a paint property) on a
   blurred element. Split into a clipping box + an inner tile that animates
   transform.
4. Canvas shadowBlur per glyph/particle per frame in MatrixRain and
   SparkleField (~6000 shadowed fills/sec each). Both now pre-rasterise to
   an offscreen sprite and blit with drawImage.
5. side-rays animated scaleX, which resizes a blurred layer and forces
   re-rasterisation of ~1.5M px nine times a frame.

THE COUNTERINTUITIVE ONE: removing 'will-change: transform' from the
background-gradient tile was worth ~30fps on its own. It reads like the
correct hint but forces every tile into a permanently retained composited
layer, and blurred layers that size are expensive to hold. Do not add it
back. There is a comment in the file saying so.

Also: 'bg-black/88' generates NO CSS rule. Tailwind's default opacity scale
runs in steps of 5, so 88 is off-scale and the panel silently had no
background at all. Verified against a standalone tailwind 3.4.19 run —
/65, /85, /90 and /[0.88] all emit, /88 does not.
